MAJOR_VERSION=5
STANDARD_VERSION=3
RELEASE_VERSION=0
PRERELEASE=1
DEV_VERSION=13
DESCRIPTION=Fix freestanding VGA console wrap (#419). fl_fs_vga_putc in kernel/drivers/freestanding_x86_64/vga.c now advances the row (and scrolls when needed) when a line reaches 80 columns, instead of resetting only the column and overwriting the same row. New make test_fs_vga_wrap covers wrap, bottom-row scroll, and newline. Serial output was already correct. make baremetal builds a bootable kernel (#423). It used to produce the hosted Linux program (BPForbes_Flinstone_Shell, dynamically linked against libc, SQLite and OpenSSL, compiled with -DDRIVERS_BAREMETAL=1 and -no-pie), which has no boot loader and no Multiboot or PVH header, so QEMU -kernel refused it. The target now builds dist/flintstone.img, the MBR, long-mode loader and freestanding kernel that make browser-kernel already builds, by sharing that path. The hosted compile check of the bare-metal driver paths moves to make baremetal-drivers (same flags as before), and AGENTS.md's validation line, the README, docs/ARCH.md, docs/P3_NETWORKING.md, docs/ROADMAP.md, docs/browser-kernel-lab.md and scripts/package_browser_kernel_artifact.sh name the right target. Fix make baremetal link failure for fl_kq_driver_get (#421). Hosted bare-metal builds still compile KeyQuorum checkpoint objects under FL_WITH_KEYQUORUM, but driver_model.c gated FL_MODEL_RESOLVES_KQ behind !DRIVERS_BAREMETAL, so fl_kq_driver_get was undefined while kq_checkpoint.o and fl_kq_driver_get_or_register still called it. The model now resolves KeyQuorum whenever FL_WITH_KEYQUORUM or FL_LAB_KQ is set; bus enumeration and register_all still skip KeyQuorum under DRIVERS_BAREMETAL. Versioning: introduce RERELEASE=1 so a prerelease row may reuse an existing A.B.C + DEV_VERSION (here 5.3.0 BUILD 13) without bumping RELEASE_VERSION; uniqueness CI and AI/docs policy updated accordingly. FL-02 stage 2: KeyQuorum-signed file histories in the freestanding image, kept on the image's data disk. The bss moves above 1 MiB (linker.ld links it at FL_KERNEL_BSS_ADDR, 0x100000, and never loads it); stage 2 refuses to boot unless one usable E820 entry holds it (FLINTSTONE_BOOT_FAIL bss-memory), the boot info reserves the image and the bss as two ranges, and fl_bootinfo_validate checks both, so the loaded image ends at 0x8bb60 (0x87440 security lab) with room below 0x9f000. The kernel stack grows to 24 KiB (0xa000). The checkpoints run in the default image over the in-memory storage table kq_mem_storage.c, and the store is persisted to the second IDE disk (kq_disk.c, ATA PIO on the secondary master): an append-only log of records, each a header sector (magic, kind, name, length, CRC-32) written last after its data sectors, read at boot before the accounts are enrolled, with a damaged record ending the log. Histories (kind 1) and sealed account keys (kind 2) persist, so a signed history still verifies after a reboot and the default accounts keep their keys. Commands: server file sign (asks for the password, opens the sealed key for that one signature, wipes it), log, verify; the image prints CAP keyquorum-disk=present|absent and CAP keyquorum-history=ok after a boot self-test. The placement layer, the shared folder gate and the container mutex are hosted-only; kq_platform.c supplies the libc subset and the clock as KeyQuorum UTC text; fl_fs_join_path and the reserved-suffix check are shared header inlines; the image trust table holds 8 signers. Tests: test_kq_checkpoint, test_fs_bootmap, make test-freestanding-kq-history, make test-freestanding-kq-persist (reboot restore, damaged record, no disk); test-freestanding-shell and test_freestanding_entry unchanged and passing. The security-lab image (SECFEAT=1) now includes the same KeyQuorum code, sealed keys, data disk and fw_cfg entropy: the build script refuses a payload over the loader limit (1144 sectors below 0x9f000) rather than a fixed 1024, and the security-lab payload is 1027 sectors (image end 0x90488). Browser lab has no secondary disk: kq_disk IDENTIFY treats a floating bus (status 0xFF) as absent immediately and caps ATA polls so Wasm QEMU does not stall past the Ready gate. KeyQuorum refusal reports stay coarse and the recipient's audit names the cause. The receive path in server file passes the checkpoint's own result to fl_kq_report_refusal_cp_in. The signed KQER report, which is carried to the sender, keeps the coarse class through fl_kq_cp_to_result (malformed as Framing, a missing key as Untrusted, too large as Denied/resource, every signature, trust, replay, freshness, fork, threshold or permit refusal as Denied/policy), so a report is not an oracle for the sender (KeyQuorum #169). The precise cause (cause=bad-signature, cause=replayed, cause=forked and the rest) is written only to the recipient's local audit line. Local problems (the destination exists, an I/O error, no driver) are still not reported. fl_kq_report_refusal_in and the checkpoint form share one writer. test_kq_service covers every cause, checks that a replay and a bad signature sign the same code and class, and checks the unreported results. Version policy: RERELEASE=1 reuses 5.3.0 DEV_VERSION=13 (BUILD 13; original is the VGA wrap row). An optional RERELEASE flag (0 or 1, after DEV_VERSION when present) marks a row that reissues an existing version on purpose: a PR stacked on another PR takes its base's DEV_VERSION in its own .ver, and a GA version may be reissued on its way to main. check_version_entries_semver_dev_unique.sh requires exactly one original (RERELEASE=0 or absent) per (MAJOR, STANDARD, RELEASE, DEV_VERSION), and RERELEASE=1 needs that original and never carries GM=1. It is temporary: when the branch carrying it merges, scripts/fold_rerelease_entries.sh appends its DESCRIPTION to the original's and deletes the re-released file from version/entries and version/locked (CI on a merged push to a feature branch, Version lock on merge on develop and main). version/locked rows carry no RERELEASE line (finalize_version_locked.sh drops it; scripts/lib/ver_locked_form.sh, which the subset check compares against), and check_version_locked_immutable.sh allows a fold, and only a fold, to change a published row. tests/test_version_scripts.sh covers each rule; CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, docs/versioning.md, replit.md, the Cursor rule and .coderabbit.yaml state it. CodeQL on PR #417: both Version lock create-pull-request steps pin peter-evans/create-pull-request to 3ffccacd46053766916f087ce41d627c0b8cf2d1 (v8.1.3) instead of the mutable @v8 tag. Bare-metal / browser-lab relay chat now prints in the guest terminal (#426), and cat/type can no longer forge lab control lines from received or written files (#427). The page types each peer message at an idle command prompt as hex in 48-byte parts (`relayrecv + <from> <hex>` ... `relayrecv <from> <hex>`) so every typed line fits the guest's 128-character buffer; the kernel joins the parts (up to the relay's 512 bytes, dropping an unfinished message on a sender change, a bad part or leaving) and prints the whole message as `[Server Message, From <from>]: <text>`, broken between words at 80 columns, with only bytes 0x20-0x7E and colour-only ANSI SGR codes (everything else `?`). relayrecv requires an armed server seat (`server host|join` or the page's quiet `server seat` / `server unseat` on connect/leave) and is refused under lab isolation. The VGA console no longer shows page plumbing: the shell keeps `relayrecv`, `filerecv`, `server seat` and `server unseat` lines off the screen (serial keeps them), and the `SERVER_RELAY msg|announce` and `SERVER_FILE` hex control lines go to serial only, so a sender does not see their message repeated. The same filter backs `cat` / `type` behind a fixed `[file] ` prefix per logical line so SWITCHUSER, SERVER_*, and LAB_NETMODE in file content never match parseGuestLine. The VGA console (bare metal and the WebAssembly shell's buffer) now draws SGR colour codes 0, 1, 22, 30-37, 39, 40-47, 49, 90-97 and 100-107 as text attributes and swallows other escape sequences; relay messages and file lines that used colour end with a reset so a sender cannot recolour the receiver's prompt, and cursor-moving or other escape sequences still show as `?`. lab-core.js exports relayRecvLine; browser-lab tests and test-freestanding-shell cover parts, wrapping, colour, the sanitize and seat rules, and test_fs_vga_wrap checks the SGR attributes; README documents the round trip. docs/COLOR_SYSTEM.md records the color system: which color each server helper uses, the ANSI escape bytes and the VGA attribute bytes (hex, binary, ASCII), the SGR codes the VGA console draws, and the files involved. The image colours its own server text on the VGA screen only (serial, redirects and the lab page's parsed lines stay plain), with the hosted helpers' tags and colours (userland/shell/fl_colors.c): chat is cyan `[Server Message, From <from>]: <text>`, `[Server]` success green and warnings yellow, `[ERROR]` red; test-freestanding-shell checks the attribute bytes. CodeQL on PR #417: drop unused import sys from scripts/test_freestanding_kq_persist.py. Browser boot Power Off: clear the page emulator handle before destroy, no-op QMP I/O after dispose, and always terminate qemu-worker.js so a racing relay seat flush cannot leave workers alive (test_browser_boot).
